1) Network:
* Interconnected devices communicate and share data with each other.

2) Hub:(layer 1)(raw bits)
* It just broadcasts the data with connected device in the local network.
* It does not store any address and has no storage.

3) Switch:(layer 2)(frames)
* It sends data to the destination via MAC address within the local network.
* It stores the MAC address in the CAM(Content Addressable Memory) table.
* When pinged another device via IP address it sends arp(Address Resolution Protocol) request.
* arp request is send to connected devices and the specific IP device returns a direct message and the MAC address is stored.

4) Router:(layer 3)(packets)
* It works by logical addressing(IPs).
* Used to connect different networks.

5) OSI:
layer-7: Application layer(data)
HTTP - Hypertext Transfer Protocol
HTTPS - Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure
FTP - File Transfer Protocol
TFTP - Trival File Transfer Protocol
DNS - Domain Name System
SMTP - Simple Mail Transfer Protocol
IMAP - Internet Message Access Protocol
DHCP - Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol
SNMP - Simple Network Management Protocol
Protocols:
* HTTP/HTTPS: Allows web browsing securely.
* FTP: Transfer files between local computer and remote server.
* TFTP: Simple, lightweight protocol to transfer files between network devices.
* SMTP/IMAP: Handles sending and retrieving emails.
* DNS: Translates domains into IP adresses.
* DHCP: Automatically assigns IP addresses, subnet masks, and default gateways to devices.
* SNMP: Used to monitor, manage and configure network devices like routers, etc.

layer-6: Presentation layer(data)
SSL - Secure Sockets Layer
TLS - Transport Layer Security
SSH - Secure Shell(Secure Socket Shell)
Protocols:
* SSL/TLS: Secure web traffic by encrypting data before transmission.
* SSH: Provides secure, encrypted remote access to computer systems.

layer-5: Session layer(data)
NetBIOS - Network Basic Input/Output System
RPC - Remote Procedure Call
PPTP - Point-to-Point Tunneling Protocol
PAP - Password Authentication Protocol
Protocols:
* NetBIOS: Provides services(sessions) for applications on different computers to communicate over a local area network(LAN).
* RPC: Allows a program on one computer to execute code on a different computer without the programmer needing to explicitly code the network details.
* PPTP: An older method used to create secure Virtual Private Network (VPN) tunnels over the internet.
* PAP: A simple, plain-text routing protocol used to validate user credentials before letting them access a network.

layer-4: Transport layer(segment/datagrams)
TCP - Transmission Control Protocol
UDP - User Datagram Protocol
Protocols:
* TCP: TCP is a reliable transmission as if any packages is failed to deliver then it resends the certain package.
* UDP: UDP just transmits the data after the connection but doesn't bother whether the reciever got all packages.

layer-3: Network layer(packets)
OSPF - Open Shortest Path First
BGP - Border Gateway Protocol
RIP - Routing Information Protocol
ICMP - Internet Control Message Protocol
IGMP - Internet Group Management Protocol
Protocols:
* IPv4: Uses 32 bit addresses to route data packets.
* IPv6: Uses 128 bit addresses to provide unique id addresses and replace IPv4.
* OSPF: Finds the fastest path within a single corporate or campus network.
* BGP: Manages  data routing between different internet service providers across the global internet.
* RIP: Old simpler protocol that counts the number of routers to find a path.
* ICMP: Sends error messages and operational data used by ping command.
* IPsec: Secures IP communications by encrypting and authenticating data packets.
* IGMP: Instead of unicast or broadcast, the network layer uses IGMP to send just one stream of data that only goes to the devices that explicitly asked for it.(multicast)

layer-2: Data link layer(frames)
PPP - Point-to-Point Protocol
HDLC - High-Level Data Link Control
ARP - Address Resolution Protocol
STP - Spanning Tree Protocol
L2TP - Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol
Protocols:
* Ethernet(IEEE 802.3): Used to transmit data frames for wired local area networks(LANs).
* Wi-Fi(IEEE 802.11): Used to transmit data frames over radio waves.
* PPP: Used to establish a direct connection between two nodes.
* HDLC: A synchronous, bit-oriented protocol used to transmit data over point-to-point leased lines in WANs.
* ARP: Translates known layer 3 IP address into a local layer 2 MAC address.
* STP: A management protocol that prevents network killing broadcast loops.
* L2TP: Used by ISPs and VPNs to tunnel and support virtual private network connections.

layer-1: Physical layer(bits)
DSL - Digital Subscriber Line
USB - Universal Serial Bus
SONET/SDH - Synchronous Optical Networking
OTN - Optical Transport Network
Standards:
* Wi-Fi Physical Layer: Handles radio frequency modulation to send bits through the air.
* Bluetooth Physical Layer: Uses short-range radio frequencies.
* Cellular Physical Layer: Manages advanced radio wave modulation techniques for mobile tower connections.
* Ethernet Physical Layer: Copper twisted-pair cables.
* Fiber Optic Standards: 
* DSL: Uses traditional telephone copper lines to transmit high frequency digital data.
* USB: Defines peripheral device connections.
* SONET/SDH: Standard for high-speed laser data transmission over long-distance fiber-optic networks.
* OTN: Modern framework used by internet providers to route massive amounts of data over fiber globally.
